// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["iv"] = {"qs": [["Trading Mechanism", "A trader changes the price of a pending limit order in the order book. What happens to its time priority?", ["It keeps its original time priority", "It gets priority over all orders at that price", "It loses its original time priority", "It is automatically converted to a market order"], 2, "Under price-time priority, a modification of price (or an increase in quantity) is treated as a fresh order and receives a new time stamp. This keeps the queue fair for orders already waiting."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A dealer tells a client, 'This IRF strategy cannot lose money; I guarantee 12% a year.' This is:", ["Acceptable if the client is a bank", "Acceptable if given in writing", "A violation of the code of conduct", "Required to help the client decide"], 2, "Brokers must not guarantee returns or make misleading statements about derivatives, which can always cause losses. Such assurances violate the SEBI code of conduct for stock brokers."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "Which money market instrument is issued by scheduled commercial banks to raise short-term funds?", ["Certificate of Deposit", "Commercial Paper", "Treasury Bill", "Cash Management Bill"], 0, "Certificates of Deposit are negotiable money market instruments issued by banks (and select financial institutions). Commercial Paper is issued by companies, while T-bills and Cash Management Bills are issued for the Government of India."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A trader buys 10 IRF contracts (face value ₹2 lakh, quoted per ₹100) at 99.00. The day's settlement price is 99.40. The mark-to-market for the day is:", ["Gain of ₹8,000", "Gain of ₹800", "Loss of ₹8,000", "Gain of ₹80,000"], 0, "MTM = 10 × 2,00,000 × (99.40 − 99.00) / 100 = ₹8,000. As a buyer, the trader gains when the settlement price rises. It is credited in daily settlement."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Before offering an OTC interest rate derivative to a retail user, a market-maker must:", ["Carry out a suitability and appropriateness assessment", "Obtain prior written approval from SEBI for each individual transaction", "Guarantee the user a minimum return", "Ensure the user holds a demat account with CDSL"], 0, "RBI requires market-makers to assess whether the product suits the retail user's needs and understanding, and to disclose risks clearly. This protects less sophisticated users from unsuitable products."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "An 8% coupon bond is trading at ₹104 (face value ₹100). Which relationship among its yields is correct?", ["Coupon rate < current yield < YTM", "Current yield < YTM < coupon rate", "YTM < current yield < coupon rate", "YTM = current yield = coupon rate"], 2, "A bond priced at a premium has a current yield (8/104 = 7.69%) below its coupon, and its YTM is lower still because the ₹4 premium is lost by maturity. For discount bonds the order reverses."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "A bank sold bond futures to hedge its G-sec holdings. Yields then rise sharply. What is the most likely overall outcome?", ["Both the bond holdings and the short futures position make losses", "Bonds gain while the short futures lose value", "Short futures lose, doubling the bond losses", "Loss on bonds is largely offset by a gain on short futures"], 3, "Rising yields push bond prices down, causing a loss on the holdings. The same move lowers futures prices, so the short futures position gains. A well-sized hedge offsets most of the loss, leaving basis risk."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "For a derivative trade on a recognised exchange to be treated as non-speculative for tax purposes, it must be supported by:", ["An auditor's certificate issued for each trade", "A bank statement showing a debit of the full notional", "A time-stamped contract note with the client's unique code and PAN", "A demat statement showing delivery of the bond"], 2, "Tax law treats eligible derivative transactions on recognised exchanges as non-speculative only when they are carried out through a registered broker and supported by a time-stamped contract note showing unique client identity and PAN."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "In bond futures, the 'gross basis' of a deliverable bond is defined as:", ["Futures price minus the bond's coupon rate", "Conversion factor minus the bond's yield", "Cash price minus (futures price × conversion factor)", "Futures price multiplied by the accrued interest"], 2, "Gross basis compares the bond's cash price with its adjusted futures price (futures × conversion factor). It reflects carry and the delivery option; the bond with the lowest net basis is usually the cheapest to deliver."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "A company plans to raise money by issuing bonds in two months and fears that yields will rise before then. Using interest rate futures, it should:", ["Buy futures now and sell them at issue", "Wait and buy futures on the issue date", "Sell futures now and buy them back at issue", "Buy call options on bond futures now"], 2, "If yields rise, the cost of borrowing rises but futures prices fall, so a short futures position gains and offsets the higher cost. Buying futures or calls would gain only if yields fell."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Initial margin paid to the broker for an IRF position is best recorded in the books as:", ["An asset, being a deposit recoverable from the broker", "An expense charged to profit and loss immediately", "A liability payable to the clearing corporation", "Part of share capital of the business"], 0, "Initial margin is money placed with the broker or clearing member as security and is refundable when positions close, so it is shown as a current asset, not as an expense."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "A deliverable bond's dirty price is ₹100. The repo (financing) rate is 6% p.a. and the bond earns coupon income at 7% p.a. Ignoring compounding, what is the theoretical 3-month futures price?", ["₹100.25", "₹101.50", "₹98.25", "₹99.75"], 3, "Futures = spot + financing cost − coupon income = 100 + (100 × 6% × 0.25) − (100 × 7% × 0.25) = 100 + 1.50 − 1.75 = ₹99.75. Because carry is positive, futures trade below spot."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A broker uses money lying in Client A's account to meet Client B's margin call. This is:", ["Permitted if both clients use the same branch", "Permitted for up to three working days", "Not permitted, as each client's funds must be used only for that client", "Permitted when the broker is a clearing member"], 2, "Client funds are held for that client's own obligations. Using one client's funds for another, or for the broker's own purposes, breaches SEBI rules on segregation and monitoring of client funds."], ["Trading Mechanism", "On NSE, 91-day T-bill futures are quoted as:", ["The discount yield expressed directly in %", "100 minus the annualised discount yield", "The face value of the T-bill in rupees", "The clean price of the T-bill per ₹1,000"], 1, "T-bill futures are quoted as 100 minus the futures discount yield, so the quote rises when yields fall. This keeps the usual futures behaviour of price moving opposite to rates."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "A ₹25 crore bond portfolio has a modified duration of 4.8. A bond futures contract has a value of ₹2 lakh and a modified duration of 7.5. How many contracts are needed to fully hedge?", ["640", "1,250", "800", "960"], 2, "Contracts = (portfolio value × portfolio MD) / (contract value × futures MD) = (25,00,00,000 × 4.8) / (2,00,000 × 7.5) = 1,20,00,00,000 / 15,00,000 = 800 contracts, to be sold."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "Which of the following is NOT an interest rate derivative?", ["10-year G-sec futures", "91-day T-bill futures", "An overnight indexed swap", "USD-INR currency futures"], 3, "Currency futures derive value from an exchange rate, not an interest rate. G-sec futures, T-bill futures and OIS all derive their value from interest rates or interest-bearing instruments."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Under RBI's framework for OTC rupee IRDs, a corporate that enters into a swap with a bank purely to hedge its loan is classified as a:", ["Market-maker", "Clearing member", "User", "Primary dealer"], 2, "RBI divides participants into market-makers (who quote prices, such as banks and primary dealers) and users (who use derivatives to hedge or manage risk). A corporate hedging its loan is a user."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Trader X opens a new long position of 10 contracts. The seller, Y, is closing out an existing long position of 10 contracts. What happens to open interest?", ["It rises by 10 contracts", "It remains unchanged", "It falls by 10 contracts", "It rises by 20 contracts"], 1, "One side opens and the other closes, so the open position simply passes from Y to X. Open interest rises only when both sides open new positions and falls when both sides close."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Initial margin on interest rate futures is generally computed using:", ["A flat 50% of the contract's notional value", "The client's annual income declared in KYC", "A portfolio-based risk model such as SPAN", "The previous day's trading volume"], 2, "Clearing corporations use portfolio-based models (SPAN-type methodology) that estimate the worst likely one-day loss across scenarios. The margin therefore reflects actual risk, with offsets for hedged positions."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "Which of these options on bond futures has zero intrinsic value?", ["A call whose strike is below the futures price", "A call whose strike is above the futures price", "A put whose strike is above the futures price", "A deep in-the-money put close to expiry"], 1, "A call has intrinsic value only when the underlying price exceeds the strike. With the strike above the futures price the call is out of the money, so its whole premium is time value."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A trading member must issue a contract note for executed trades to the client within:", ["7 working days of the trade", "The end of the calendar month", "The expiry date of the contract", "24 hours of the trade"], 3, "Contract notes, physical or electronic, must reach the client within 24 hours of execution. They record trade details and charges and serve as legal evidence of the transaction."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "In which money market segment do participants borrow and lend against government securities through a tri-party arrangement, with CCIL as the central counterparty?", ["TREPS", "Call money market", "Notice money market", "Commercial paper market"], 0, "TREPS (Tri-party Repo Dealing and Settlement) is a collateralised segment where CCIL acts as the tri-party agent and central counterparty. Call and notice money are uncollateralised interbank loans."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "The price a buyer actually pays for a bond bought between two coupon dates is the:", ["Clean price, i.e. the quoted screen price without any adjustment", "Face value adjusted for the coupon rate", "Clean price minus the accrued interest", "Dirty price, i.e. clean price plus accrued interest"], 3, "Bonds in India are quoted at clean price, but settlement happens at the dirty (full) price, which adds interest accrued since the last coupon date. The seller is thus paid for the interest earned while holding the bond."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Spreading false information to move bond futures prices for personal gain would violate:", ["SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations", "SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations", "The Foreign Exchange Management Act's current account rules", "The Banking Regulation Act's licensing provisions"], 1, "The PFUTP Regulations prohibit manipulative and deceptive practices in the securities market, including spreading misleading information to influence prices of exchange-traded derivatives."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A trader holds a put option on bond futures. The option gains value when interest rates:", ["Fall, because bond futures prices rise", "Stay unchanged until expiry", "Fall, because put premiums move with rates", "Rise, because bond futures prices fall"], 3, "Bond prices move opposite to yields. When rates rise, bond futures fall, so a put on bond futures moves into the money. A call on bond futures is the instrument that gains when rates fall."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A buyer pays a premium of ₹1.20 for a call option on a bond with a strike price of ₹100 (per ₹100 face value). Ignoring costs, the break-even price at expiry is:", ["₹98.80", "₹101.20", "₹100.00", "₹102.40"], 1, "A call buyer breaks even when the underlying price equals strike plus premium: 100 + 1.20 = ₹101.20. Below that the option does not recover its cost. ₹98.80 is the break-even for a put buyer."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A sell stop-loss order on IRF with a trigger price of 98.50 becomes active when:", ["The market price rises to 98.50 or higher", "The trading session ends at the closing bell", "Open interest crosses a preset limit", "The market price falls to 98.50 or lower"], 3, "A sell stop-loss is used to cap losses on a long position. It stays inactive until the price drops to the trigger, at which point it is released as a limit or market order."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "Simultaneously buying the near-month contract and selling the far-month contract of the same interest rate futures is called a:", ["Butterfly spread", "Calendar spread", "Basis trade", "Straddle"], 1, "A calendar spread uses two expiries of the same contract. Its margin is usually lower than for outright positions because most of the price risk offsets. A basis trade combines a cash bond with futures."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "A '3×9 FRA' refers to an agreement covering:", ["A 9-month period starting 3 months from today", "A 6-month period starting 3 months from today", "A 3-month period starting 9 months from today", "A 12-month period starting 3 months from today"], 1, "FRA notation gives the start and end months from today. A 3×9 FRA starts in 3 months and ends in 9 months, so it fixes the rate for a 6-month period beginning three months ahead."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "In an overnight indexed swap (OIS), the floating leg payment is usually calculated by:", ["Using the 3-month term rate fixed once at the start of the reset period", "Averaging the 10-year G-sec yield across the period", "Compounding the daily overnight reference rate over the period", "Taking only the overnight rate on the payment date"], 2, "In an OIS the floating leg reflects the overnight rate compounded for every day of the reset period. In India the common benchmark is the overnight MIBOR, so the floating leg tracks actual daily funding costs."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A clearing member's main role in the derivatives segment is to:", ["Design and list new interest rate futures contracts for the exchange", "Settle the obligations of trades executed by its trading members", "Set the policy repo rate for the market", "Issue government securities on behalf of RBI"], 1, "Clearing members meet funds settlement obligations with the clearing corporation for trades of their own and of the trading members whose trades they clear, and they collect margins from them."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Before a client starts trading in exchange-traded derivatives, the trading member must provide the client with:", ["A guarantee that losses will be limited", "The Risk Disclosure Document explaining derivative risks", "A copy of the exchange's annual report", "The trading member's own income tax return"], 1, "Exchanges and SEBI require members to give clients a Risk Disclosure Document covering leverage, margin calls and possible losses, so the client understands derivative risks before trading."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "A fund manager wants to reduce the duration of a bond portfolio quickly without selling bonds. The manager should:", ["Buy interest rate futures", "Buy call options on bond futures", "Enter an IRS to receive fixed", "Sell interest rate futures"], 3, "Short futures have negative duration, so adding them lowers the portfolio's overall duration. Buying futures or receiving fixed in a swap would increase duration instead."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A put option on bond futures has a strike of 99.00 while the futures price is 97.50. Its intrinsic value is:", ["1.50", "0.00", "97.50", "3.00"], 0, "Put intrinsic value = strike − underlying price, if positive: 99.00 − 97.50 = 1.50. Any premium above 1.50 is time value. A call with the same strike would have zero intrinsic value."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A 'payer swaption' gives its holder the right, but not the obligation, to:", ["Enter into a swap receiving the fixed rate", "Cancel an existing floating-rate loan", "Enter into a swap paying the fixed rate", "Buy a bond at a fixed future price"], 2, "A swaption is an option on an interest rate swap. A payer swaption lets the buyer pay fixed (and receive floating), which is valuable if rates rise. A receiver swaption gives the right to receive fixed."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A borrower's cap has a strike of 7% on a notional of ₹10 crore. For a quarterly period (0.25 year) the reference rate is set at 8%. The caplet payoff is:", ["₹10,00,000", "₹25,00,000", "₹2,50,000", "₹25,000"], 2, "Payoff = notional × (reference rate − cap rate) × period = ₹10 crore × 1% × 0.25 = ₹2,50,000. ₹10 lakh ignores the quarterly period fraction."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Position limits on interest rate futures for clients and members are mainly meant to:", ["Guarantee a minimum daily profit to every participant in the contract", "Fix the maximum brokerage a member can charge", "Restrict trading to institutional investors only", "Prevent excessive concentration and market manipulation"], 3, "Position limits stop any single participant from building a dominant position that could distort prices or create settlement risk. They are a key part of the risk containment framework."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Stock exchanges and clearing corporations on which interest rate futures are traded are regulated by:", ["Reserve Bank of India", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "Clearing Corporation of India Ltd", "SEBI"], 3, "SEBI regulates market infrastructure institutions such as stock exchanges and clearing corporations, including their exchange-traded interest rate derivative segments, while RBI sets the broader policy for IRDs."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "A company has issued fixed-rate debentures but now expects interest rates to fall. Through an interest rate swap, it can benefit from falling rates by:", ["Receiving fixed and paying floating", "Paying fixed and receiving floating", "Paying fixed and receiving fixed", "Buying a forward rate agreement"], 0, "Receiving fixed offsets its fixed coupon outflow, leaving it effectively paying floating. If rates fall, its floating payments drop. Paying fixed would lock in the high cost, which suits a borrower fearing rising rates."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A dealer learns of a large client order to sell bond futures and first sells for the firm's own account. This practice is called:", ["Hedging", "Arbitrage", "Netting", "Front-running"], 3, "Trading ahead of a known client order to profit from its price impact is front-running. It misuses confidential client information and is prohibited."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "When a buyer pays premium for an interest rate option, the premium is initially recorded as:", ["Income in the profit and loss account", "A reduction in the bank loan balance", "Part of the borrower's equity reserve", "An asset, until the option is exercised or expires"], 3, "The premium buys a right, so it is carried as an asset (measured at fair value). It is adjusted on exercise, sale or expiry, with any loss reflected in profit or loss."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Under the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956, a contract for differences or an instrument deriving value from an underlying is treated as a:", ["Negotiable instrument", "Deposit", "Commodity", "Security"], 3, "The SCRA's definition of 'securities' includes derivatives. This is why exchange-traded derivatives, including interest rate futures, fall within SEBI's regulatory framework."], ["Trading Mechanism", "An order placed without any price limit that executes at the best price available in the order book is a:", ["Market order", "Limit order", "Stop-loss order", "Good-till-cancelled order"], 0, "A market order seeks immediate execution at the best available price. It offers certainty of execution but not of price. A limit order sets the worst acceptable price."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "Which of these bonds will have the highest duration, assuming all yields are similar?", ["A 10-year bond paying a 9% coupon", "A 5-year zero-coupon bond", "A 10-year zero-coupon bond", "A 10-year floating rate bond"], 2, "A zero-coupon bond's duration equals its maturity, so the 10-year zero has duration of about 10 years. A coupon bond of the same maturity has lower duration, and a floating rate bond resets close to market, so its duration is very short."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Running-account settlement of an active client's unused funds by the broker must be done:", ["Periodically, at least quarterly or monthly as the client opts", "Only when the client finally closes the trading account with the broker", "Once every two years after an audit", "Only if the client files a written complaint"], 0, "SEBI requires brokers to settle unused client funds on a periodic basis according to the client's choice of monthly or quarterly settlement, so client money does not stay idle with the broker."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "On exchange-traded interest rate options, margins are mainly collected from:", ["Option writers, since buyers pay the full premium upfront", "Option buyers, since writers have already received the premium in cash", "Both sides equally, as a fixed share of notional", "Neither side, because options are fully pre-paid"], 0, "A buyer's maximum loss is the premium already paid, so no further margin is needed. A writer's potential loss is large, so the clearing corporation collects margins from the seller to cover adverse moves."], ["Exchange Traded Interest Rate Options", "A borrower buys a cap at 7.5% for a premium of 0.40% and sells a floor at 5.5% for a premium of 0.30% on the same notional. The net upfront cost of this collar is:", ["0.70% of notional", "0.40% of notional", "0.30% of notional", "0.10% of notional"], 3, "Net cost = premium paid − premium received = 0.40% − 0.30% = 0.10%. The floor income lowers the cost of protection, in exchange for giving up gains if rates fall below 5.5%."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "Bond futures are trading below their theoretical fair value. The reverse cash-and-carry arbitrage is to:", ["Sell the futures, buy the bond in the cash market and fund it via repo", "Buy the futures, short-sell the bond and invest the proceeds", "Buy both the futures and the bond at the same time", "Sell the futures and hold cash until expiry"], 1, "When futures are cheap, the arbitrageur buys futures and sells the bond in the cash market, investing the proceeds (reverse repo). At expiry the bond is received under the futures and returned, locking the mispricing."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "A zero-coupon bond with face value ₹100 matures in exactly 2 years. If the annual yield is 8% (annual compounding), what is its price?", ["₹84.00", "₹85.73", "₹92.59", "₹86.21"], 1, "Price = 100 / (1.08)^2 = 100 / 1.1664 = ₹85.73. ₹84.00 wrongly deducts simple interest of 16%, while ₹92.59 discounts for only one year."], ["Trading Mechanism", "A Professional Clearing Member (PCM) in the derivatives segment:", ["Clears and settles trades of trading members but does not trade", "Trades on its own account and for clients but cannot clear any trades", "Only provides price quotes as a market maker", "Manages investor grievances for the exchange"], 0, "A PCM is a clearing-only entity, typically a bank or custodian, that clears and settles trades executed by trading members tied up with it. It does not execute trades on the exchange."], ["Regulatory Framework", "RBI's power to regulate interest rate derivatives comes mainly from:", ["The Companies Act, 2013", "Chapter III-D of the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934", "The Depositories Act, 1996", "The Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881"], 1, "Chapter III-D of the RBI Act gives RBI powers over interest rate derivatives, money market instruments and related transactions. RBI's Rupee Interest Rate Derivatives Directions are issued under these powers."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "An inverted yield curve means that:", ["Long-term yields are higher than short-term yields", "Yields are the same for every maturity bucket", "Corporate bond yields are below G-sec yields", "Short-term yields are higher than long-term yields"], 3, "An inverted curve slopes downward: short-tenor yields exceed long-tenor yields. It often signals expectations of falling rates or slower growth. A normal curve slopes upward."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "At inception, the value of a fairly priced interest rate swap to each counterparty is:", ["Equal to the notional amount", "Zero", "Equal to one fixed-leg payment", "Positive for the fixed payer only"], 1, "The fixed rate in a fair swap is set so that the present value of the fixed leg equals the present value of the expected floating leg. Value arises later only as market rates move away from the contract rate."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A bank hedges the change in fair value of its fixed-rate bond holdings by selling interest rate futures. Under Ind AS 109, this is a:", ["Cash flow hedge", "Net investment hedge", "Fair value hedge", "Speculative position"], 2, "A fair value hedge protects against changes in the value of a recognised asset or liability. Hedging the price of fixed-rate bonds against rate moves is a classic fair value hedge."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A client fails to pay the mark-to-market loss on IRF positions. Who remains liable to the clearing corporation for the settlement obligation?", ["The stock exchange on whose platform the trades were originally executed", "SEBI, as the market regulator", "The clearing member through whom the trades are cleared", "The issuer of the underlying government security"], 2, "The clearing corporation deals only with clearing members, who must meet obligations on time whether or not their clients pay. The member then recovers dues from the trading member or client."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Open interest in a futures contract represents:", ["The total number of contracts bought and sold during the whole trading day", "The number of clients holding a demat account", "The number of outstanding contracts not yet closed or settled", "The total margin deposited with the clearing corporation"], 2, "Open interest counts positions still open at the end of a period. Volume counts all trades in the period, including those that open and close positions on the same day."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A trader closes out an IRF position held for trading purposes. The cumulative gain on the position is recognised:", ["In profit and loss", "Directly in share capital", "As a contingent liability", "Only when the trader files a tax return"], 0, "Futures held for trading are measured at fair value through profit or loss, so gains and losses flow into the statement of profit and loss, both daily through MTM and on final close-out."], ["Regulatory Framework", "Investment limits for foreign portfolio investors in exchange-traded interest rate futures are prescribed by:", ["RBI", "SEBI alone", "The stock exchange board", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 0, "Because IRF positions relate to the government debt market and capital flows, RBI sets the limits for FPI participation. SEBI and exchanges then implement monitoring of those limits."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "You believe the gap between 10-year and 2-year G-sec yields will narrow because 10-year yields will drop more. Which futures position fits this view?", ["Buy long-tenor bond futures and sell short-tenor futures", "Sell long-tenor bond futures and buy short-tenor futures", "Sell both long-tenor and short-tenor futures equally", "Buy only short-tenor futures and hold to expiry"], 0, "If long yields fall relative to short yields, long-tenor futures will outperform. Buying them and selling short-tenor futures profits from the flattening while reducing exposure to a parallel shift."], ["Trading Mechanism", "Every client trading in exchange-traded IRDs must be identified on the trading system through a:", ["Demat account number of the trading member", "Bank account number of the clearing member", "Contract note number issued after trading", "Unique Client Code linked to the client's PAN"], 3, "Exchanges require every order to carry a Unique Client Code mapped to the client's KYC details, including PAN. This enables surveillance, position monitoring and correct allocation of trades."], ["Interest Rate Derivatives", "In a plain vanilla rupee interest rate swap, what is exchanged between the parties at the start of the contract?", ["Nothing; the notional amount is not exchanged", "The full notional amount, paid by each party to the other", "The first year's interest paid in advance", "A margin equal to 10% of the notional amount"], 0, "The notional only serves to calculate interest payments. In a single-currency IRS it is never exchanged, and on each payment date only the net difference between fixed and floating interest changes hands."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Why is margin on a calendar spread position usually lower than on two outright positions?", ["The long and short legs largely offset each other's risk", "Spread positions cannot make any loss", "Calendar spreads are not marked to market", "Clearing corporations ignore far-month contracts"], 0, "In a calendar spread the two legs move closely together, so the net risk is much smaller than that of two independent positions. Margin models recognise this with a lower spread charge."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A business loss from eligible exchange-traded interest rate derivative trading, treated as non-speculative, can be carried forward for up to:", ["4 assessment years", "8 assessment years", "2 assessment years", "Only the same year"], 1, "Non-speculative business losses can be carried forward for up to eight assessment years and set off against business income. Speculative losses have a shorter carry-forward period."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "The Settlement Guarantee Fund (Core SGF) of a clearing corporation is used to:", ["Pay brokerage to trading members", "Fund new product launches by the exchange", "Compensate investors for market losses", "Complete settlement when a member defaults"], 3, "The Core SGF is a pool that ensures trades settle even if a clearing member fails to meet obligations. It is used after the defaulter's own margins and contributions in the default waterfall."], ["Interest Rates & Fixed Income Market", "A bond has a modified duration of 7 and convexity of 60. If its yield falls by 100 basis points, its approximate percentage price change is:", ["+6.70%", "+7.30%", "+7.00%", "+7.60%"], 1, "Change ≈ −MD × Δy + ½ × convexity × (Δy)² = −7 × (−0.01) + 0.5 × 60 × 0.0001 = 7% + 0.30% = +7.30%. Ignoring convexity gives 7.00%, which understates the gain."], ["Regulatory Framework", "An entity wishing to deal for clients in exchange-traded interest rate futures must be:", ["Licensed by RBI as a scheduled commercial bank with a treasury desk", "Registered with IRDAI as a corporate agent", "Registered with SEBI as a stock broker and be an exchange member", "Approved by AMFI as a distributor"], 2, "Trading members in the exchange IRD segment are stock brokers registered with SEBI and admitted as members of the exchange. Bank status or AMFI registration is not what permits trading for clients."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A retired client with no derivatives experience asks to take large leveraged IRF positions after hearing a tip. The broker's most appropriate step is to:", ["Execute the order immediately, since the client has clearly asked for it in writing", "Explain the risks, assess suitability and record the advice given", "Refuse to open any account for the client", "Promise to reverse any losses within a week"], 1, "Brokers should ensure the client understands leverage and margin calls, check whether the product fits the client's profile and keep records. Refusing outright or blindly executing are both poor practice."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "A company uses an IRS to fix the interest it will pay on a floating-rate loan. Under a cash flow hedge, the effective portion of the swap's fair value change is recognised in:", ["Retained earnings directly", "Share premium account", "Revenue from operations", "Other comprehensive income"], 3, "In a cash flow hedge, the effective part of the derivative's gain or loss is taken to OCI (cash flow hedge reserve) and later reclassified to profit or loss when the hedged interest affects earnings."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Under Ind AS 109, hedge effectiveness is assessed mainly by checking that:", ["Hedge results stay within a fixed 80%–125% offset range at every single reporting date", "An economic relationship exists and the hedge ratio reflects actual risk management", "The hedging instrument is always traded on an exchange", "The hedge produces a profit in every reporting period"], 1, "Ind AS 109 dropped the rigid 80–125% test of the older standard. Instead it requires an economic relationship, credit risk not dominating value changes, and a hedge ratio consistent with actual risk management."], ["Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "The Investor Protection Fund (IPF) of a stock exchange is used mainly to:", ["Pay eligible claims of investors against a defaulting trading member", "Make good the market losses suffered by investors on their open trading positions", "Fund the exchange's advertising campaigns", "Pay incentives to brokers with high volumes"], 0, "When a trading member is declared a defaulter, the IPF compensates eligible investor claims up to set limits. It does not cover losses from adverse market movements."], ["Strategies Using Interest Rate Derivatives", "A trader sold 50 bond futures contracts (face value ₹2 lakh each, quoted per ₹100) at 99.20 and bought them back at 98.40. Ignoring costs, the result is:", ["Profit of ₹80,000", "Profit of ₹8,000", "Loss of ₹80,000", "Profit of ₹8,00,000"], 0, "Price fell by 0.80 per ₹100. Profit = 50 × 2,00,000 × 0.80 / 100 = ₹80,000. Being short, the trader gains when the price falls; ₹8,00,000 forgets to divide by 100."], ["Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "For a cash-settled IRF, the final settlement price at expiry is derived from:", ["The last traded price of any equity index", "Prices or yields of the underlying security in the cash market", "The opening price of the contract on its first day", "A price chosen by the client before expiry"], 1, "Cash-settled contracts close by paying the difference between the trade price and a final settlement price, computed from prices or yields of the underlying G-sec or T-bill in the cash market."]]};
